Stress test for the shorthand-before-longhand order: 30 rules in one context
(the bare atom class), four genuine shorthand/longhand families - `padding`,
`margin`, `border-width`, `inset` - and sixteen unrelated singleton
declarations shuffled between them once, with a fixed seed. No block orders
them, so only cover edges ("a shorthand before a longhand it covers in the
same context") and definition order decide. The sheet is built from its end:
every rule stays at its definition position unless a cover edge pulls a
shorthand ahead of a longhand defined before it.

`border-width` covers `border-top-width` and `border-left-width`; the cover
test compares the longhand sets (`Slot_key.leaves_of`), so no depth number is
involved.

